  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nTODAY:\n\nAs of 115 AM CST, lake-effect snow along the South Shore has\nbriefly paused as thermal profiles show some near-surface\ndrying, further evidenced by observed surface dewpoint \ndepressions of 7-11F. This should be short-lived as increased \nlow-level moisture returns this morning through early evening, \nbringing back light lake-effect snow to parts of the South \nShore (i.e., portions of Bayfield County and Price County, and \nprimarily in Ashland and Iron counties). Much of this low-level \nmoisture remains outside of the dendritic growth zone, just \ntouching it at the top of the layer with much drier air above \nit. Therefore, snow rates will continue to be light, \nparticularly when combined with warm ground temperatures and \nabove-freezing air temperatures mid- morning through the \nafternoon today. Expect additional accumulations to largely be \nless than 0.5\", though 0.5\" to around 1\" are still possible in \nthe higher terrain of the Gogebic Range in Ashland and Iron \ncounties.\n\nOutside of the South Shore lake-effect snow and associated cloud\ncover into northwest Wisconsin, most of the remainder of the\nNorthland will be dry with a mix of clouds/clear skies early and\nbecoming mostly sunny this afternoon. High temperatures today\nremain slightly below average, topping out in the mid to upper \n30s alongside light north to northeast winds.\n\nTHIS WEEKEND THROUGH EARLY NEXT WEEK:\n\nA several day period of dry weather sets up for this weekend\ninto early next week as upper-level ridging moves over the\ncentral CONUS/Upper Midwest. High pressure settles into the \nregion for this weekend, with light and variable winds Saturday\nand southerly winds on Sunday. Temperatures gradually warm\nstarting this weekend, first with highs in the upper 30s to low\n40s on Saturday and low to mid 40s on Sunday. Overnight lows\ndrop into the mid teens to 20s. Expect mostly sunny skies both\ndays.\n\nMonday and Tuesday will also be dry with even warmer\ntemperatures as the surface high pressure slides east of the\nregion and breezier southerly winds usher in high temperatures\nin the mid 40s to low/mid 50s.\n\nMID TO LATE NEXT WEEK:\n\nNo significant changes to the previous forecast as a larger low\npressure system is on track to bring increasing precipitation \nchances Thursday/Friday of next week as it moves into the\nNorthern Plains and Upper Midwest. Well-above normal\ntemperatures continue through the latter half of next work week\nwith highs in the upper 40s to mid 50s and lows in the mid 30s\nto around 40 degrees. Given these warmer temperatures, the\nfavored precipitation type is rain for the Northland.",
